  "articleBody": "It's easy to think installing new cabinet pulls is a simple DIY, but a few common missteps can really throw a wrench in your project. One biggie is skipping pilot holes or drilling them at the wrong angle. This can lead to splintered wood or pulls that aren't quite straight, and trust me, those mistakes are tough to hide without replacing the whole cabinet door! Another common issue is overtightening screws. You might think you're making it secure, but you could actually strip the threads or even crack the wood, especially with softer materials. Sometimes, folks also drill holes too close to the edges of the cabinet, which can weaken the wood and look pretty off-kilter. And don't forget about checking the actual pull size against your existing screw spacing. If you just assume they'll match without measuring the center-to-center distance, you might end up with extra holes or pulls that just don't line up. When these kinds of errors pop up, especially on expensive cabinetry, it's often a good idea to bring in a professional like HH Remodeling LLC to help sort out the damage or tackle the installation correctly from the start.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
